      "result": "All 19 checklist items have been tested and recorded — the run is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\n**11 PASS / 8 FAIL**\n\n**Passing:** search & filtering (FT-1), model detail navigation (FT-2), node dragging (FT-3), template library browsing (FT-6), scenario switching (FT-9), simulation run/reset (FT-10), value clamping (CS-12), live coverage updates (IX-13), graph rendering/pan/zoom (CT-15), risk-gap creation & clearing (CT-17), gap alert banner (CT-18).\n\n**Key failures — most trace back to one root cause:** the graph component seeds React Flow via `useNodesState`/`useEdgesState` and never re-syncs, so its nodes, edges, highlighting, and change-handler closures are frozen at first render.\n\n- **IX-20** — Node controls show stale values (still \"Advanced\" after setting Beginner), and each edit is applied to the *original* model snapshot, silently discarding the previous edit.\n- **FT-5** — Disabled competencies show no visual state, and a second toggle can never re-enable them.\n- **FT-7** — Applying a template updates the header/metrics but leaves the previous model's nodes and edges on the canvas, so graph and summary depict different jobs.\n- **CT-16** — Critical path includes disabled competencies, isn't a traversable directed path, and graph highlighting never recalculates.\n\n**Independent gaps:**\n- **FT-4** — No mechanism of any kind to edit relationship (edge) weights; they're read-only labels.\n- **FT-8** — \"Save Current\" is a dead button; no name/description form and no My Templates collection exists.\n- **FT-11** — Save shows a success toast but writes nothing to storage; edits are lost on leaving the model.\n- **IX-19** — The default Recruitment scenario loads with all adjustments zeroed, so running it without switching tabs does nothing.\n\nTwo notes on method: the pan check (CT-15) initially appeared to fail because Playwright's element-centered drag kept landing on overlapping nodes and edge hit-areas rather than empty canvas — panning does work once the drag starts from a clear point, so I judged it a PASS. Conversely, for CT-17 the \"disabling clears the gap\" path is confounded by the stale-state bug: disabling also resets the level, so the warning clears either way. The observed outcome matched expectations, so I recorded PASS, but that specific sub-behavior isn't independently confirmed.",
